  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nAfter a week of above normal temperatures, temperatures this\nafternoon were close to normal for late October with readings in \nthe middle 40s to middle 50s. Regional radars were indicating \nscattered showers across north-central and central WI westward \ninto southwest and west-central WI. \n\nLow pressure across the central plains will move northeast into\nMinnesota late tonight, then to western Lake Superior Tuesday\nafternoon. Weak 850mb and a weak impulse were producing the \nshowers across Wisconsin early this afternoon. The scattered \nshowers will continue for the rest of the afternoon into tonight, \nwith rain chances at 40-80% with the highest across central and \nnorth-central WI. The showers should come to an end Tuesday across\nthe north as the system pulls away from the area.\n\nSoutherly winds will bring in warmer temps on Tuesday, with some \nof the warmest temperatures this week on Wednesday where highs \nshould reach the upper 60s to middle 70s. A cold front will bring \nanother round of showers and a few thunderstorms (rain chance \n50-80%) Wednesday night. Slightly cooler, but still well above \nnormal temperatures are expected on Thursday. A few showers may \nlinger into the morning hours on Thursday, otherwise high pressure\nwill build into the region. Dry conditions will prevail Thursday \nnight into Friday morning before the next chance of rain arrives \nFriday afternoon. It appears the upcoming weekend will be \nunsettled with chances of rain throughout the weekend. It will be \nmild with readings well into the 50s to lower to middle 60s. It \nshould be noted the normal high for November 1st is around 50 \ndegrees across the area, and will also be the day we set the \nclocks back one hour as we switch to Central Standard Time.",
